According to its graph, the one-sided limits at $x=-3$ are different for function $f$ and therefore $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -3}f(x)$ doesn't exist.

However, before we jump to any conclusions regarding $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -3}\Big(f(x)+h(x)\Big)$, let's consider the one-sided limits:

$\begin{align}
&\blueD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -3^-}f(x)=3}
\\\\
&\purpleD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -3^+}f(x)=0}
\\\\
&\maroonD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -3^-}h(x)=\lim_{x\to -3^+}h(x)=2}
\end{align}$

We can find each one-sided limit of $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -3}\Big(f(x)+h(x)\Big)$ using the fact that the limit of a sum of functions is the sum of the individual limits:

$\begin{align}
&\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -3^-}\Big(f(x)+h(x)\Big)=\lim_{x\to -3^-}f(x)+\lim_{x\to 3^-}h(x)
\\\\
&\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -3^+}\Big(f(x)+h(x)\Big)=\lim_{x\to -3^+}f(x)+\lim_{x\to 3^+}h(x)
\end{align}$
